# Template for the production environment file. The real
# server/.env.production is NOT committed — the deploy workflow materializes
# it on the VM from the PROD_ENV_B64 secret stored in the GitHub
# "production" environment:
#   base64 < server/.env.production | gh secret set PROD_ENV_B64 --env production
#
# This file carries EXACTLY the two names the app stack's server consumes
# via its env_file (docker-compose-prod.yml) — nothing else:
#   SDG_JWT_SECRET              the HS256 signing key (application.yml rail:
#                               spring.security...jwt.secret-key)
#   SPRING_DATASOURCE_PASSWORD  the PostgreSQL password
#
# SINGLE-SOURCE WIRING: the platform stack's postgres consumes the SAME
# SPRING_DATASOURCE_PASSWORD via interpolation
# (docker-compose-platform.yml: POSTGRES_PASSWORD=${SPRING_DATASOURCE_PASSWORD:?...}).
# The deploy/CI shell runs `set -a; . server/.env.production; set +a` before
# any compose command, so ONE value feeds both the database and the app —
# they cannot disagree.
#
# The placeholder values below must BOOT the real app outside dev: the
# server's ProdSanityConfig refuses the prod profile on dev-grade values
# (the application.yml dev-default JWT key, the 'guide' dev datasource
# password), so both carry long, clearly-non-secret placeholder strings
# that pass that check. SPRING_DATASOURCE_URL/USERNAME and the mongo/redis
# hosts stay unset in this FILE on purpose — docker-compose-prod.yml
# supplies them via its environment: block, which beats env_file, pointing
# at the platform datastores by network alias.
SDG_JWT_SECRET=placeholder-jwt-signing-secret-32-plus-bytes-ci-and-docs-only
SPRING_DATASOURCE_PASSWORD=placeholder-db-password-not-a-secret-ci-and-docs-only
